## 4. Apply the Design Consistently Across Current Templates

Inventory all existing generated document types before deciding the patch scope. At minimum, identify whether the application currently generates invoices, quotations, payment receipts/acknowledgments, statements, and project reports.

### Invoice

Apply the reference design most faithfully to the invoice, while preserving all existing invoice fields and accounting semantics.

### Quotation

Reuse the shared visual language (brand/header, metadata alignment, party panel, project callout, typography, currency presentation, restrained separators, and footer) where the existing quotation template supports it.

Preserve quotation-specific fields such as quotation number, issue/expiry dates, scope of work, options, exclusions, terms, acceptance/signature area, and itemized pricing where present. Do not label a quotation as an invoice or show invoice-only payment status.

### Receipt / payment acknowledgment

Reuse branding, typography, metadata, party hierarchy, and currency alignment. Preserve payment-specific information such as receipt/reference number, payment date, payment method, amount received, related invoice, balance after payment, and acknowledgment wording where those fields exist.

Do not imply that a payment acknowledgment is a legally compliant tax document unless the application’s supported requirements have been confirmed.

### Statement or project report

Use shared tokens and header/footer conventions if appropriate, but prioritize each document’s specific information architecture. Do not force invoices’ financial table or party block into documents where it makes the content harder to understand.

### Shared styles

If multiple templates use the same styling, introduce or extend shared CSS tokens/components only when this reduces duplication without causing unrelated layout regressions. Keep document-specific styles scoped to avoid one template unintentionally changing another.

